  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None; full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T5",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates downstream performance across protein language model scales and reports that larger baseline models do not reliably outperform smaller ones. It also reports that reverse distillation improves scaling in many tested settings, refining the scope of scaling claims rather than testing pretraining-loss power laws directly.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports ProteinGym DMS and protein-property prediction outcomes across model sizes. These bear on downstream scaling (V6), but it does not measure held-out cross-entropy, fit loss power laws, predict large-run loss from small runs, or test compute-optimal allocation. Related-work claims about other studies are not recorded as rows.",
    "test_reason": "Among its own results, the paper reports downstream probe performance of off-the-shelf ESM-2 models at several sizes (8M to 15B; Tables 1 to 4). It argues that protein language models do not improve reliably with scale. This bears on V6 in a new setting (protein LMs, linear and ridge probes). The reverse-distilled (rd) results come from the paper's own post-hoc method, which builds in nested prefixes by design. They are not evidence about scaling as it arises naturally.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors say PLM scaling challenges stem from inefficient use of representational capacity, not limits of expressiveness. They say a linear decomposition restores monotonic scaling and beats baselines at equal embedding dimension. Note that their claim of baseline degradation leans partly on cited work: their own Table 3 baseline means mostly rise with size.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study decomposes protein-model embeddings using smaller models as a basis and orthogonal residuals; it does not fit held-out language-model loss scaling laws or optimize training compute allocation.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Downstream scores differ by task and model scale; the paper also reports effects of reverse-distillation chain configuration, but those results are not extracted here.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Post-hoc reverse-distilled embeddings (nested prefixes built from smaller models) rise nearly monotonically with target size and beat the baselines on most tasks.",
        "proposed_claim": "A linear decomposition of representations across model scales can restore monotonic downstream scaling in protein LMs.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r2",
          "r4",
          "r6",
